    "content": "Mogadishu — The Somali Federal Government and the South West State administration have issued separate statements regarding the fighting that erupted in Baydhabo, the capital of Bay region, on September 28, 2026. The two sides offered differing accounts of who was responsible for the clash and the current security situation.\n\nThe Federal Ministry of Defense stated that the attack was launched by forces loyal to South West State leader Abdiaziz Hassan Mohamed Laftagareen, along with fighters from the militant group Al-Shabaab. According to the ministry, federal troops and South West State forces mounted a strong defense and pushed back the assailants.\n\nThe South West State administration, however, provided a different narrative, though details of their statement were not fully articulated in the source material. The conflicting claims underscore the ongoing tensions between the federal government and the regional administration, which have periodically clashed over security and political issues.\n\nBaydhabo has been a focal point of instability in recent years, with Al-Shabaab frequently targeting the city and its surroundings. The involvement of Laftagareen's forces in the reported attack, as claimed by the federal government, highlights the deepening rift between Mogadishu and the South West State leadership.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the latest violence in Baydhabo is a stark reminder of the persistent insecurity in parts of the country. Many diaspora members have family ties to the Bay region and closely follow developments there. The conflicting reports may also complicate efforts by diaspora organizations to support peace and development initiatives in the area.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/28/dowladda-federaalka-iyo-koonfur-galbeed-oo-war-ka-soo-saaray-dagaalkii-baydhabo/",
