    "summary": "Omar Abdirashid Ali Sharmarke, a former prime minister of Somalia, has called for agreement on federal elections and addressed constitutional amendments and elections in federal member states.",
    "content": "MOGADISHU, Somalia — Former Somali Prime Minister Omar Abdirashid Ali Sharmarke has publicly addressed the country's political situation, emphasizing the need for consensus on federal elections. In remarks reported by Horseed Media, Sharmarke focused on constitutional amendments, the conduct of elections in Somalia's federal member states, and the importance of reaching an agreement on the federal electoral process.\n\nSharmarke served as prime minister of Somalia from 2009 to 2010 and again from 2014 to 2017. He has remained involved in political affairs and is the son of Abdirashid Ali Sharmarke, Somalia's second president, who was assassinated in 1969. His latest comments come amid ongoing debates over Somalia's electoral framework and the balance of power between the federal government and member states.\n\nSomalia has struggled to hold direct, one-person-one-vote elections for decades due to security challenges and political disagreements. In recent years, indirect elections have been used, but disputes over the process have often delayed the formation of government. The federal member states—including Puntland, Jubaland, Galmudug, Hirshabelle, South West, and Banadir—have varying degrees of autonomy and influence over local electoral arrangements.\n\nConstitutional amendments have also been a contentious issue. In 2023, Somalia's bicameral parliament approved amendments to the provisional constitution, including the introduction of a four-year term for the president and a shift toward a presidential system. Some federal member states and opposition figures have raised concerns about the process, calling for broader consultation.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, these political developments are closely followed. Many diaspora members remit funds, invest in businesses, and engage in political advocacy. Uncertainty over elections can affect stability, economic confidence, and the ability of diaspora communities to participate in Somalia's political future, particularly regarding representation and voting rights.\n\nSharmarke's call for agreement reflects broader concerns that without consensus, electoral delays or disputes could further destabilize the country. As Somalia prepares for future elections, the dialogue between federal authorities and member states will be critical in determining the credibility and inclusiveness of the process.\n\nRead the original report from Horseed Media here: https://horseedmedia.net/former-somali-prime-minister-calls-for-agreement-on-federal-elections/425967/",
