    "slug": "puntland-asserts-right-to-decide-on-foreign-bases",
    "summary": "Puntland's information minister stated the regional government has the authority to negotiate foreign military bases on its territory, amid federal government talks with the UAE.",
    "content": "Puntland's Minister of Information, Najiib Barkhadle Warsame, held a press conference in Garoowe on [date not specified], declaring that the regional administration has the right to consult on and make decisions regarding military bases within its borders. The statement comes as the federal government of Somalia engages in discussions with the United Arab Emirates about establishing a military facility in the country.\n\nThe minister's remarks underscore Puntland's position that any agreement affecting its territory must involve its input. Puntland, a semi-autonomous region in northeastern Somalia, has long asserted its autonomy in local governance and security matters. The federal government, based in Mogadishu, has been negotiating with the UAE on a base that could be located in Puntland or elsewhere.\n\nThis development is part of ongoing tensions between Puntland and the federal government over resource and security management. In recent years, Puntland has frequently voiced opposition to federal decisions that it perceives as encroaching on its regional authority. The UAE has previously trained Somali forces and expressed interest in expanding its military presence in the Horn of Africa.\n\nThe Somali diaspora, particularly those from Puntland, is closely monitoring this issue as it affects regional stability and international partnerships. Many diaspora members have investments and family ties in Puntland, and any foreign military presence could influence local politics and security. The diaspora often contributes to debates on Somali sovereignty and federalism, and this statement may spark further discussion within diaspora communities.\n\nAs the federal government continues its negotiations, Puntland's assertion of its rights could complicate the process. The outcome will depend on how the federal and regional authorities reconcile their positions, with potential implications for Somalia's federal structure and foreign relations. The international community, including the UAE, will likely watch these developments closely.\n\nRead the original report from Horseed Media here: https://horseedmedia.net/puntland-oo-ku-dhawaaqday-inay-xaq-u-leedahay-ka-tashiga-aayeheeda/425959/",
